WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.094
A practical secrets management routine starts before the secret is created.

2
00:00:05.094 --> 00:00:12.226
Identify whether the value grants access, proves identity, signs data, encrypts or decrypts information,

3
00:00:12.226 --> 00:00:16.928
connects to a protected system, or allows one service to trust another.

4
00:00:16.928 --> 00:00:20.377
If it crosses a trust boundary, handle it as a secret.

5
00:00:20.377 --> 00:00:24.687
Classify it by environment, scope, privilege, lifetime, owner,

6
00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:28.841
and impact so the team knows how carefully it must be protected.

7
00:00:28.841 --> 00:00:31.897
Store secrets in approved protected systems.

8
00:00:31.897 --> 00:00:36.600
Use a secret manager, vault, KMS-backed service, HSM-backed service,

9
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:43.967
or managed platform feature that provides access policy, audit trails, versioning, and operational support.

10
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:46.710
Avoid hardcoding secrets in source code.

11
00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.393
Avoid plaintext configuration that is broadly copied.

12
00:00:50.393 --> 00:00:54.155
Avoid unmanaged scripts and local files that no one owns.

13
00:00:54.155 --> 00:00:59.798
A secret outside the lifecycle is a future incident waiting for the right conditions.

14
00:00:59.798 --> 00:01:02.463
Restrict access using least privilege.

15
00:01:02.463 --> 00:01:06.381
Give humans and workloads only the secret access they need.

16
00:01:06.381 --> 00:01:10.065
Scope service accounts and pipeline tokens carefully.

17
00:01:10.065 --> 00:01:15.943
Prefer managed identity and short-lived credentials where the platform supports them.

18
00:01:15.943 --> 00:01:20.880
Review access regularly and treat broad vault access as broad system risk.

19
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.993
Keep break-glass access available for emergencies, but make it auditable and exceptional.

20
00:01:26.993 --> 00:01:30.755
Deliver secrets safely to applications and automation.

21
00:01:30.755 --> 00:01:38.044
Fetch secrets at startup or runtime through approved mechanisms, mount them through trusted orchestrators,

22
00:01:38.044 --> 00:01:42.276
or replace static credentials with managed identity patterns.

23
00:01:42.276 --> 00:01:47.448
Be cautious with environment variables and understand where they may appear.

24
00:01:47.448 --> 00:01:51.915
Keep CI/CD secrets scoped, masked, approved, and out of artifacts.

25
00:01:51.915 --> 00:01:55.442
Above all, do not log secrets or return them in errors.

26
00:01:55.442 --> 00:01:57.323
Plan for change.

27
00:01:57.323 --> 00:02:00.458
Rotate secrets on a schedule when appropriate.

28
00:02:00.458 --> 00:02:02.731
Use expiration to limit lifetime.

29
00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:08.687
Revoke secrets immediately when exposure is suspected or access is no longer authorized.

30
00:02:08.687 --> 00:02:11.822
Test rotation procedures before emergencies.

31
00:02:11.822 --> 00:02:17.386
Know which services depend on each secret and how to verify that a replacement works.

32
00:02:17.386 --> 00:02:22.324
A rotation process that nobody trusts will be delayed when it matters most.

33
00:02:22.324 --> 00:02:24.205
Monitor and respond.

34
00:02:24.205 --> 00:02:29.926
Scan repositories, pipelines, logs, artifacts, and images for accidental exposure.

35
00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:32.512
Review audit logs for access patterns.

36
00:02:32.512 --> 00:02:40.036
When a leak happens, revoke or rotate, investigate the exposure path, search for reuse, update affected systems,

37
00:02:40.036 --> 00:02:44.895
communicate responsibly, document the timeline, and improve controls.

38
00:02:44.895 --> 00:02:50.303
The final lesson is simple: secrets management is a lifecycle, not a hiding place.

39
00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:57.435
Strong programs combine storage, access control, delivery, rotation, monitoring, response, ownership,

40
00:02:57.435 --> 00:02:59.394
and continuous improvement.
